<h2>The block-page rhythm</h2>

<p>Every block page (one per day of class, ~80 minutes each) follows the same five-section template:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Story.</strong> What today is about and why it matters. The connection back to the unit's anchor question.</li>
  <li><strong>Concepts.</strong> Headed "Before you start the work". Watch, read, interact. The line under the heading always opens with one of four instructions, and it means exactly what it says. See the table below.</li>
  <li><strong>Do.</strong> Today's work. A choice board, usually 4 options that all build toward the same Learning Intention.</li>
  <li><strong>Show.</strong> What you're submitting today and how it's scored.</li>
  <li><strong>Reflect.</strong> One question before you leave. No grade. Builds the metacognitive close that makes the next block easier.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The four openings in Concepts</h3>

<p>Whatever the opening says, it describes only the cards you choose between. Anything under <strong>"Everyone does this"</strong> is not part of the choice: the unit vocabulary page, your AI partners, and the frameworks you keep coming back to are available on every route.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>If the line starts</th><th>It means</th><th>You should</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Pick one</strong></td><td>The cards cover the same ground.</td><td>Choose whichever suits you. One is enough.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Start with the Watch</strong> (or Read, or Interact, or Make)</td><td>That card is the day's anchor and everyone does it.</td><td>Do the named card first. The rest are there afterwards.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Use all of these</strong></td><td>Nothing here substitutes for anything else.</td><td>Work through all of them, in any order.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Keep these open</strong></td><td>A reference shelf, not a menu.</td><td>Reach for them while you do today's work.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Do not confuse this with the <strong>Do</strong> section further down. Concepts is how you meet the material; Do is the work itself. A Do line always begins "All four paths end the same", because there you pick one route to a single shared artifact. A Concepts line never does.</p>

<p>Every block page also has an <strong>AI Partners callout</strong> at the top, the partners relevant for that day's work, and a <strong>bottom navigation</strong> with the previous block, the unit map, and the next block.</p>

<h2>Finding things quickly</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Looking for today's work?</strong> Top nav → Course Units → your unit → today's block page.</li>
  <li><strong>Looking up a term?</strong> Top nav → Reference → Glossary.</li>
  <li><strong>Need the rubric while writing?</strong> Top nav → Student Hub → Rubric Quick Reference.</li>
  <li><strong>Need to fill out AI Documentation?</strong> Top nav → Student Hub → AI Documentation Template.</li>
  <li><strong>Forgotten how OPVL works?</strong> Top nav → Foundations → OPVL Framework.</li>
</ul>

